A||N||15||Seanh||What is this?||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-09-2000||07:50 PM||||I was putting my engine back together from trying to find my coolant leak when I stumbled across a loose sensor (I think). It is on the right side of the intake manifold. V6 engine might I add. It goes straight down into the engine. There is a ring around the part that stays on the engine, and it is broken. It is round and it is located under the little hose the comes out of the right side of the manifold. Twords the front of the car, right in front of the thermo housing. It has one wire, Darker Green with a White strip going into it. What the heck is it? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000001||ka4nkf||10-09-2000||08:00 PM||||sounds like the coolant fan switch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-09-2000||09:03 PM||||I think that is what it is. Would it leak coolant if it had physically broken? I hope so much I have found my leak.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-09-2000||09:31 PM||||Hey, I just took it all the way out. It looks like what the Haynes manual calls the coolant temperature sensor. If the plastic on the top of mine were broken, would it leak coolant? I think this might be my problem because it seems that this is the exact area coolant is comming from. I cant be for sure because I was putting to engine back together to start and run the car to find my leak, and I found this broken. Any Ideas? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||10-09-2000||09:35 PM||||If I am not mistaken both coolant sensors (cold start and coolant temp) are sealed brass as far as anything that would "touch" antifreeze.   I do not think that is the leak...    I had a problem with one of the two little hoses right there that run coolant to the throttle body having a tiny leak when at operating temperature.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-09-2000||09:48 PM||||Yes, that is right, it is brass. The top is plastic and broken. I dont think this is the leak because I cant seem to blow thru it, so I doubt it would leak coolant. I think I have a pretty large leak because I was just going down the road and noticed the car overheating, so I pulled over, poped the hood and saw smoke comming off the front manifold. I looked under the car and saw a puddle of coolant under the A/C compressor. I now see some old coolant up in the area of the broken sensor. I think it is a pretty large leak, not a small leak the wouldnt be visable. I just wonder why I can see the stupid thing. Oh well, Ill replace this and run the car to see if I can find where its comming from. Thanks1<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2000||09:56 PM||||That's the switch allright. If only the plastic ring is broken don't think it would leak. If it was loose as well, it does have a source for coolant as it's base is in the cooling system passage. Need to replace it anyway if broken!<BR>Make sure that you use white teflon tape on the new threads so it seals good.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||10-09-2000||10:09 PM||||DON'T USE TEFLON TAPE ON THE SENSOR!!!!! The brass shell of the sensor must be grounded to the block in order for the fan switch to work.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-09-2000||10:12 PM||||I was thinking of that, but my experience with teflon tape is that is will be cut on the egdes, so it sould provide good enough grounding, dont you think?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2000||10:16 PM||||Good call Seanh! That's what it does for sure. Then it gets to engine temp and seals in a heartbeat. I use it all the time, no troubles.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||10-09-2000||10:16 PM||||I always the teflon pipe sealer in a tube. Works great on everything from steam pipe fittings to head bolts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-09-2000||10:33 PM||||Would it be okay to run my car with putting the thermostat back in? Just to check for leaks, I was thinking this would allow it to run coolant without waiting for the car to get up to operating temp. This way I could check for leaks without waiting for the car to get hot in this cold weather. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||862M4inCA||10-10-2000||05:30 AM||||yes, it's perfectly ok to run the engine without the thermostat to check for leaks, I just wouldn't recommend driving the car long distances without it since it allows the water to move too quickly through the radiator and doesn't dissipate as much heat as it was designed to. However, it's possible that this leak would not occur until the system is under pressure, in which case you would still have to run the car long enough to warm it up and build pressure in the cooling system. Otherwise I would think that your leak would occur when your cooling system is full. <P>you could also try pressurizing the cooling system. There is a tool, I believe I've seen it available through Snap-On tools, or if you have a Harbor Frieght Tools store near by, they might have one. It's just a pump that pressurizes your cooling system to allow you to check for a leaking cooling system. That should work to help push the coolant out and allow you to locate your leak. I would also check the two hoses that Greg refered to. Those sound like a VERY good possibility. Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pontiaddict||10-10-2000||07:02 PM||||Check the water pump bolts. (And most of the front cover bolts if I remember) Also, look at the pipe on top of the water pump going to the heater core hoses. That's where mine leaks. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||ROB MAC||10-11-2000||02:48 PM||||Next time the car is hot and leaking get a flashlight and look all around the head gasket for a tiny trickle of coolant.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||10-11-2000||05:13 PM||||I am not the best machanic, but I can tell you this is no little trickle of coolant, more like a waterslid of coolant the way it was pouring out of the car. I havent ran it since it overheated, but I am going to work on it tonight. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
